        "Paris, the capital and largest city of France, sits along a sweeping bend of the River Seine in the heart of the northern Île-de-France region. Known around the world as the \"City of Light\" (La Ville Lumière)—both for its pivotal intellectual role during the 18th-century Age of Enlightenment and because it was one of the first European capitals to illuminate its boulevards with gas street lamps—Paris has stood for centuries as a global crossroads of art, literature, philosophy, architecture, fashion, and gastronomy.",
        "Beginning as a Celtic settlement of the Parisii tribe on the Île de la Cité around the 3rd century BCE, the Roman town of Lutetia grew into the medieval seat of the Capetian kings, witnessing the construction of Notre-Dame Cathedral and the founding of the University of Paris (the Sorbonne) in the 12th century. In the mid-19th century, Emperor Napoleon III and his prefect Baron Georges-Eugène Haussmann transformed the crowded medieval streets into the iconic Paris seen today: uniform cream-colored Lutetian limestone facades, wrought-iron balconies, zinc mansard roofs, grand parks like the Jardin du Luxembourg and Bois de Boulogne, and wide tree-lined boulevards radiating from plazas such as the Place de l'Étoile.",
